+# BLOCKED: the 1 ms SLA in `test_sla` is not achievable
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_sla` requires `top_words()` to process the 4 MB reference corpus
+(600,000 words, 4,083,137 characters) in **under 1 ms**. The function now
+returns the correct result, but no pure-Python implementation can meet that
+time on this machine, so the timing assertion fails.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+The test's own reference answer is computed outside the timer with:
+
+```python
+Counter(text.lower().split())
+```
+
+That is one of the cheapest possible ways to tokenize and count this corpus in
+CPython, and it takes **~58-60 ms** here. `top_words()` cannot be faster than
+the work needed to lowercase, tokenize, and count the same 600,000 tokens,
+regardless of how the counting is implemented.
+
+Measured on this machine (Python 3.12.5, arm64 macOS, best of 7 runs):
+
+| Operation | Time |
+|---|---|
+| `text.lower()` only | ~1.5 ms |
+| `text.lower().split()` | ~21 ms |
+| `Counter(text.lower().split())` | ~58 ms |
+| optimized `top_words()` (regex + Counter + heap) | ~98 ms |
+| `test_sla` budget | 1 ms |
+
+Just lowercasing the 4 MB string already exceeds the entire 1 ms budget
+(~1.5 ms), before any tokenization or counting. Reaching 1 ms would require
+processing roughly 4 GB/s of text and producing/hashing 600,000 tokens in that
+time, which CPython cannot do.
+
+This is a hardware/language floor, not a remaining algorithmic inefficiency.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+- Rewrote `top_words()` to use `collections.Counter` and `heapq.nsmallest`,
+  replacing the previous O(words x vocabulary) list scan. On the reference
+  corpus this reduced the runtime from an impractical scan (minutes) to
+  ~98 ms while preserving exact behavior.
+- Verified correctness against the test's reference value: `got == want` holds.
+- Did **not** weaken, skip, or edit the test, cache/precompute results, use a
+  background thread, or otherwise game the timer.
+
+## Test status
+
+```
+test_basic                  ok
+test_case_and_apostrophes   ok
+test_ties_alphabetical      ok
+test_sla                    FAIL (correct result; 0.0987 s vs 0.001 s limit)
+```
+
+Everything except the impossible timing assertion passes.
+
+## Possible resolutions (require a decision outside this change)
+
+1. Raise the SLA to a realistic value for CPython (the current implementation
+   is ~100 ms; the theoretical `Counter`-based floor is ~60 ms).
+2. Accept a native extension (C/Rust) implementation. Even then, a 1 ms budget
+   for 600,000 tokens is extremely aggressive and would need separate
+   benchmarking and packaging work.
+3. Reduce the reference corpus size so 1 ms is proportionate.
